{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"4ed871b19e65eb5320d6ad240771cb841f832fc82b8d53c744784185f579214c","slug":"preface","location":"/front_matter/preface.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Preface","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Mario Medone","given":"Mario","family":"Medone"},"name":"Mario Medone","github":"Brosss98","id":"contributors-Users\\mario\\OneDrive\\Documenti\\E-Book\\machine-learning-with-python-book\\myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true},"code":{"id":"MIT","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT","name":"MIT License","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/Brosss98/machine-learning-with-python-book","keywords":["machine learning","deep learning","Python","linear algebra","scikit-learn","PyTorch","Jupyter notebooks","textbook"],"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"preface.md","url":"/machine-learning-with-python-book/_public/preface-2f1f3f918f35d511f88a757d7c238a1f.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why this book","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"IksHw2cphs"}],"identifier":"why-this-book","label":"Why this book","html_id":"why-this-book","implicit":true,"key":"rv2TgnQsTI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Machine learning is usually taught in one of two ways. In the first, it is a collection of recipes: import a model, call ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"D9R6yheFkC"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fit","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"ZwEQgMSIS9"},{"type":"text","value":", call ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"ZVxeP7LI1A"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"predict","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"kRIC0uZHZe"},{"type":"text","value":", and hope for the best. In the second, it is an abstract theory in which the connection with practice is hard to see. This book sits between the two. It assumes that you already know linear algebra and calculus, and it uses them as the language in which machine learning is explained and then checks every statement with code.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"OB71SUCrpD"}],"key":"kNjr7p23Of"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a reader with that background, most of machine learning is less foreign than it first appears. Ridge regression is a linear system with one small modification. Principal component analysis is the singular value decomposition applied to centered data. Training a neural network is the chain rule applied systematically to a long composition of functions. The aim of this book is to make such connections explicit, to explain the intuition behind each formula, and to let you verify the result on a computer.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"rSdCZELHlq"}],"key":"fNRLk3aoiK"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Who this book is for","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"iDv5LXu8v8"}],"identifier":"who-this-book-is-for","label":"Who this book is for","html_id":"who-this-book-is-for","implicit":true,"key":"u4C65S4CJV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The book is written for university students who are starting out in machine learning. It assumes:","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"GjQIwV2eCO"}],"key":"jS1rGCGL0Q"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Linear algebra:","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"QU4zKILt4n"}],"key":"EmaPIPifzE"},{"type":"text","value":" vectors, matrices, dot products, eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and the singular value decomposition.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"ah4hWr1Ya6"}],"key":"OxeC7UBZXw"}],"key":"P0JDGdSuxB"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calculus:","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"LShQhdH56r"}],"key":"w7OydvBkXF"},{"type":"text","value":" derivatives, partial derivatives, gradients, and the chain rule.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"s4qMcjCD0L"}],"key":"n9A1Hdu1n2"}],"key":"S8c4lm1gx8"}],"key":"lbIoVtp6HX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"No previous knowledge of machine learning is required. Some familiarity with programming helps. Chapter 2 reviews the Python tools used throughout the book (NumPy, pandas, and matplotlib) together with the small amount of probability that the later chapters need. Advanced mathematics, such as measure theory, is not required, and proofs are given only when they help to build intuition.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"wZqJRnrVmZ"}],"key":"OyCGeGZ8iL"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you will find","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"MZs3gUzE46"}],"identifier":"what-you-will-find","label":"What you will find","html_id":"what-you-will-find","implicit":true,"key":"k4CHIozPop"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every technical chapter is built around the same ingredients:","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"LH4GfeCPfU"}],"key":"GBLW0goYn3"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning objectives and intuition","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"dYKoMrqadC"}],"key":"B6fUAGtilV"},{"type":"text","value":", so that you know where the chapter is going and why.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"V3E8K9fk58"}],"key":"Jck8zw26fA"}],"key":"HH4QQ3BS64"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"l